      "Lean constructs a faithful barycentric incidence graph that retains loops, parallel edges, and open branches, then constructs its universal tree as based reduced walks with stepwise cancellation. The endpoint projection is a combinatorial graph covering. The actual pointed connected Galois levels constructed in B(G) are consumed directly: no semigraph, connectedness proof, deck group, action, or transition is accepted as an output field. At every verticial level the complete composite deck group retains the full automorphism group of the Galois object, including symmetries invisible on incidence data, and couples it to the universal-tree permutation. Its action is faithful on incidence plus constituent fiber data, transitive over the source incidence graph, countable under the finite-base hypotheses, and discrete. The complete deck group now acts on the lifted vertices and edges of each universal semigraph level, preserves their source projections, and has orbits exactly equal to source vertex and edge fibers. Both vertex and edge actions are also lifted to the literal geometric constituent actions: each reindexes the selected component copy and applies the retained finite Galois symmetry inside that copy, with both coordinates computed explicitly. These actions commute with finite branch comparison and lifted incidence, so they preserve the literal branch-to-edge gluing and define genuine endomorphisms of the geometric universal-cover object. Pointed refinement gives a surjective equivariant incidence map and a functorial group homomorphism of composite deck groups commuting with the canonical reduced-walk map. Lean forms the literal inverse limit of these deck groups with the initial topology from its discrete coordinates. Replacing each level by the actual image of the corresponding limit projection constructs, without added data, an inverse system of surjections of countable discrete groups; its limit is proved isomorphic and homeomorphic to the literal deck-group limit. Finite intersections of projection kernels form the identity-neighborhood basis, componentwise operations are continuous, and restriction along an initial/cofinal level functor is a topological group equivalence. Cofilteredness now turns every finite intersection in this basis into one sufficiently fine coordinate kernel. Each canonical image coordinate is surjective, so every connected continuous inverse-limit action descends to one discrete deck level and restriction along that coordinate recovers the original action. This formalizes the action-side passage to the limit in Proposition 3.6(ii). This proves the temperedness assertion of Proposition 3.6(i), including the isolated-edge case. The comparison records the source's cofinal-system independence without asserting stricter canonicity than the paper's up-to-inner-automorphism convention. Lean now also constructs the literal countable constituent-cover category B^cov(G), its associated covering semigraph and projection, and the corrected Definition 3.5(ii) componentwise finite-splitting property as a full geometric subcategory B^temp(G). The finite category B(G) embeds fully faithfully into both. Conversely, every geometric cover with finite constituent carriers is reconstructed constituentwise in the original anabelioids; its edge gluing lifts through finite temperification, and the resulting object of B(G) maps isomorphically to the given cover. Thus the finite part is recovered essentially surjectively as well as fully faithfully. Retained compatible finite Galois symmetries construct the pointed tempered-to-profinite homomorphism itself, with an explicit coordinate formula and a proof of continuity; the map is not supplied as input data. Residual finiteness separates every nontrivial kernel loop in an explicitly realized finite graph cover, proving the comparison injective; transport of the target fiber identification changes its representative by the displayed inner conjugation. The standard associated-quotient construction turns a finite-level deck action into a literal geometric tempered quotient, and root torsor normalization recovers every geometric morphism uniquely. Arbitrary corrected tempered covers decompose into intrinsic connected components, each component selects its own finite Galois level, and their countable coproduct reconstructs the original cover. Conversely, inverse-limit actions decompose into connected orbits, each orbit descends independently to a finite coordinate, and the resulting quotient covers form a lawful functor. Morphisms are recovered orbitwise through common finite refinements, proving this functor full and faithful. For essential surjectivity, the independently selected component actions are pulled back to the inverse limit and assembled as a countable disjoint action; its orbits are canonically the original geometric components. Common-refinement invariance identifies every resulting orbit quotient with its classified component, and the coproduct comparison recovers the original geometric cover. The resulting action-cover functor is packaged as an equivalence with explicit reverse functor, unit, counit, naturality, and both triangle identities.",
